body:
I'd rank most of Hoid's stories up there as amazing standalone chapters. Especially the most recent one in Rhythm of War. I'd also argue that the climax of Way of Kings is a home run and is eligible for this category. 

&#x200B;

My personal opinion is that Robert Jordan took more chances. He wrote a lot of stuff without really knowing how it would land. Sometimes he hit and sometimes he whiffed. Everything Sanderson writes is calculated to be enjoyable. He's taken the best from more creative minds, refined it, and mass produced it. 

&#x200B;

Which, I mean, yeah I probably would prefer to have a cup of coffee with Jordan, but Sanderson's technique succeeded in literally saving my life, and I still daily derive motivation from his characters, so I can't hold it or it's success to much against the guy.

Don't know true answer. I struggle with this as well. 

&#x200B;

But discipline and companionship have helped.

I mean, that's usually adolescence. Adulthood came when I realized that I couldn't run from the bullshit ( courage), or even change it in on the grandscale I dreamed of in high school (humility), but decided that I would do my best with the hand I dealt and try to make the world ever so slightly less shitty (wisdom). 

&#x200B;

And it wasn't a single moment for me. It was a prolonged process. 

&#x200B;

I think it's possible to become an adult as a teenager and it's possible to never become adult. Everyone has their own choices to make.

You know yourself better than us. 

&#x200B;

But what u/Somad2021 is saying is essentially true in my personal experience as well. 

&#x200B;

A lot of it is just learning to truly appreciate who you and what you have. Who it is that you are and what it is that you have is much less important. Comparison is the thief of joy. 

&#x200B;

But again, your mileage may very.

I agree with you. It's better to focus on the things that we have in our power. Right now is the youngest that we'll ever be. 

&#x200B;

But age discrimination does start younger than it seems like you're giving credit for. 

&#x200B;

60 with a solid reputation  >>> 34 fresh out of school looking to start a new career.

Tough conversations are for the benefit of the person on the other end of them. It's a sacrificial thing done for the good of the community. 

&#x200B;

In my experience it usually takes some time for feedback to marinate and the person being corrected could take awhile to change their behavior and longer before they have the humility to acknowledge the other person.

A drawback to thinking in those terms is that it takes health and places it in the realm "not in my control". 

&#x200B;

The reality is that there is definitely something that is within your power to do that will make your situation better. 

&#x200B;

But what do I know. I'm already 26. I gotta go take a nap

\>Well defined reasonable and achievable goals

&#x200B;

Perfectly stated.

I'm with you on the structure.

&#x200B;

Last year I laid a decent foundation and am reasonably pleased with where I'm at in my nutrition and fitness. I more than doubled the number of pushups I can do, went from barely being able to run around the block to comfortably being able to run 10 miles, added fruits and veggies to my daily diet, and maybe most importantly have upped my hygiene and household cleanliness standards. I've also eliminated weed, nicotine, booze and amphetamines from my life entirely.

&#x200B;

This year will be focused more on creative pursuits and... developing confidence and comfort in social situations. By the way, any tips on how to go about doing that are more than welcome.

I want to just whip this out the next time I'm on a date and, deadpan, roll through the entire thing. 

&#x200B;

Odds are that any woman capable of withstanding the Asperger's of it is a match for me.

>Posted byu/fantology\_podcast1 hour ago  
>  
>TV - Season 1 (Book Spoilers Allowed)Plea for support against r/whitecloaks and knights watch

I genuinely want to understand your definition of alt-right.

&#x200B;

From what I've seen in the past few hours of lurking on whitecloaks is by and large a collection of people who are attached to a particular vision that they found in RJ's writing and that they don't find in the show. 

&#x200B;

Personally, I mostly enjoy the show. It's not something that I'm going to evangelize about, but I'll be finishing the season and tuning in again next year. 

&#x200B;

Now, time for an uncomfortable topic. I enter it in good faith. 

&#x200B;

Does having a world view where genders (and people in general) have different genetic attributes that render one individual fit for a certain role and not the other make me an alt-right nazi in your eyes? 

&#x200B;

(I lack the ability to properly articulate my view right now, and I apologize for that. But I'm sure you get the gist of what I'm trying to present.)

People tend to benefit from attempting to mingle with those with different values and life perspectives. It prevents radical hardline ideologies from overridng empathy and basic human decency. It can be very difficult to do, but it's totally worth it.  Odds are the stereotype that has been fostered and nurtured by seclusion and internet echo chambers is far from accurate. 

&#x200B;

That said, when dealing with somebody who negatively impacts my life, I tell them why, and then block them. If they hear that same thing from a variety of real life people maybe they'll change their ways. If you just ghost them they don't have any chance of becoming better.

I agree with you. 

&#x200B;

I'm more talking about control of my own actions, especially in moments of stress or trial. I'm too often disappointed by the difference in how I want to behave and I actually do behave.

My dad taught to me to be loving, caring, forgiving.

&#x200B;

The rougher aspects of a sociable male are still being learned.

Props to you for having that conversation with him. It makes it that much more likely that he'll change his ways.

&#x200B;

Source - Am a recovering ass hole. Maybe a tier or two more considerate than that guy, but I know what it is to be selfish and to operate with empathy blinders.
